.TH RF-CLIP 1 "August 2026" "rf-clipboard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rf-clip \- copy stdin to an encrypted shared clipboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B command
|
.B rf-clip
.br
.B rf-clip init
.RB [ \-f ]
.br
.B rf-clip paste
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rf-clip
uploads standard input to a shared clipboard server, synced across your
devices. Content is encrypted on the client with AES-256-GCM before upload;
the server only ever stores ciphertext under an anonymous account ID.
.B rf-copy
is an alias for
.BR rf-clip .
.PP
Retrieve the clipboard with
.BR rf-paste (1)
(or
.BR "rf-clip paste" ).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Generate a random 256-bit secret and write the config file. Refuses to
overwrite an existing config unless
.B \-f
is given, because losing the secret orphans the server-side clipboard.
The server URL defaults to the hosted instance; override it with the
.B SERVER_URL
environment variable.
.TP
.B paste
Download and decrypt the clipboard to standard output. Equivalent to
.BR rf-paste (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SERVER_URL
Server URL used by
.BR "rf-clip init" ,
e.g.
.BR SERVER_URL=https://clip.example.com " rf-clip init" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rf-clipboard.conf
Configuration (default
.IR ~/.config/rf-clipboard.conf ),
written with mode 0600. Contains the server URL and the secret. Copy this
file to another device to share the clipboard with it.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings in the config file, one
.B key=value
per line:
.TP
.B server
Clipboard server URL.
.TP
.B secret
The 256-bit secret, 64 hex characters.
.TP
.B system_clipboard
When
.BR true ,
copying also writes the plaintext to the local system clipboard using the
first available tool among
.BR termux\-clipboard\-set ,
.BR pbcopy ,
.BR wl\-copy ,
.BR xclip ,
.BR xsel ,
and
.BR clip.exe .
Best-effort: a missing or failing tool prints a warning but does not fail
the copy. Default
.BR false .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Copy on one machine, paste on another:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ echo 'hello world' | rf-clip
$ rf-paste > file.txt
.RE
.fi
.SH SECURITY
One secret yields two independent values via HKDF-SHA256: the account ID
sent to the server (a hash, revealing nothing) and the encryption key,
which never leaves the client. A compromised server yields only ciphertext.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rf-copy (1),
.BR rf-paste (1)
.PP
.UR https://github.com/RunFridge/rf-clipboard
.UE
